  "id": "DrugBank_DB00169 - MONDO_1566",
  "verbalization": "Cholecalciferol, also known as vitamin D3, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hypercalcemia Disease through a series of biochemical pathways involving the conversion of precursor molecules into active forms that influence calcium homeostasis. The evidence suggests that Cholecalciferol is linked to Hypercalcemia Disease via the intermediate metabolite dihydrotachysterol, which is involved in the regulation of calcium levels in the body. This connection is supported by multiple pathways, including the involvement of osteomalacia and rickets, both of which are conditions associated with impaired calcium metabolism and are linked to dihydrotachysterol. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a strong, mechanistic relationship between Cholecalciferol and the disease, with the core mechanism likely involving the modulation of calcium homeostasis through the metabolic conversion of precursor molecules. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of dihydrotachysterol as a critical mediator in the pathophysiology of Hypercalcemia Disease, emphasizing the importance of understanding the metabolic cascade initiated by Cholecalciferol.",
